Phase 0 – Research & Mapping (do this before writing almost any code)

Goal: Understand exactly what Mario Kart 7 expects when it tries to go online.

Deliverables:
List of every hostname the game contacts
What each service does (auth, matchmaking, ranking, etc.)
Rough packet flow for login → lobby → race
Whether the actual race traffic stays on the server or goes peer-to-peer after matchmaking
Existing public resources (Pretendo’s MK7 server, NEX/PRUDP docs, Wireshark captures if available)
Phase 1 – Local Gateway Foundation (the critical first floor)

Goal: Make a real 3DS or emulator successfully log in and reach a lobby using only your local PC. No Freenet yet.

This is the most important phase. Everything else depends on it.Phase 2 – Basic Lobby Visibility

Goal: Two local gateways (or one gateway + one real Pretendo connection for testing) can see each other’s lobbies.Phase 3 – Freenet Discovery Layer

Goal: Replace the temporary discovery method with Freenet contracts for publishing and finding lobbies.Phase 4 – Gameplay Traffic Transport

Goal: Decide and implement how the actual race packets move (pure Freenet, hybrid P2P + Freenet discovery, or volunteered relays).

Only start this after you understand MK7’s real traffic pattern.Phase 5 – Hardening & Usability

Goal: Better NAT handling, anti-spam on the Freenet side, simple configuration UI, packaging so other people can run the node easily.Phase 6 – Expansion

Goal: Support a second game, then a third, while keeping the architecture clean.Recommended Starting Point for Phase 1 (the foundation)Start with this single, focused goal:Build the smallest possible local DNS + authentication stub that lets a 3DS (or Citra/Azahar) pass the Mario Kart 7 login wall and reach the online menu / lobby screen.This is the healthiest and most crucial first floor because:
It proves the DNS redirection works.
It forces you to confront the hardest part early (faking the auth/ticket responses).
It gives you a working feedback loop (you can actually test on hardware or emulator).
Everything later (Freenet discovery, gameplay transport, etc.) builds on top of a console that can already talk to your software instead of Nintendo’s.
Concrete first tasks inside Phase 1:
DNS Server only
Make a tiny DNS server that answers the known MK7 hostnames with your PC’s local IP.
Point the 3DS at that IP and confirm the console is hitting your machine instead of Nintendo.

Capture the real traffic
Use Wireshark (or similar) while the 3DS tries to go online.
Record what it sends during the login sequence.

Minimal Auth Stub
Implement the absolute minimum responses needed so the console stops getting error codes and reaches the online menu.
Use Pretendo’s open-source MK7 server as a reference, but start as small as possible — don’t copy the whole thing.

Success criteria for Phase 1
3DS (or emulator) launches Mario Kart 7
Goes online without an immediate error
Reaches the multiplayer / lobby screen
All of this happens while talking only to your local gateway

Do not touch Freenet, lobby announcements, or gameplay packet forwarding until the above works reliably.
